(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-ent-pathophysiology-quiz"]={"title":"ENT Pathophysiology Quiz 1 — Clinical Pathophysiology I","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What are vocal cord nodules, and what causes them?","choices":["Unilateral pedunculated fluid-filled growths on the middle third, from a single severe strain","Bilateral symmetrical calluses at the anterior third junction, from chronic phonotrauma","Bilateral wart-like exophytic lesions from viral infection","Unilateral firm masses with cord fixation, from malignancy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Repeated slamming of the cords causes basement membrane hyalinisation &mdash; literally a callus. Bilateral and symmetrical, because both cords take the same repeated impact at the same point. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which set of causes belongs to sensorineural rather than conductive loss?","choices":["Cerumen impaction, otosclerosis, otitis media and tympanic membrane perforation","Cholesteatoma, exostoses, foreign body and ossicular necrosis","Eustachian tube dysfunction, middle ear effusion and stapes fixation","Presbycusis, ototoxic drugs, noise trauma and acoustic neuroma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All four damage the cochlea or the nerve. Note acoustic neuroma sits here because cranial nerve VIII is part of the sensorineural pathway even though the tumour is outside the cochlea. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What kind of immune reaction is allergic rhinitis, and what does it look like?","choices":["IgG-mediated type 2 hypersensitivity with purulent rhinorrhoea","IgE-mediated type 1 hypersensitivity, with CLEAR rhinorrhoea","Immune complex deposition, type 3 hypersensitivity","T cell mediated, type 4 delayed hypersensitivity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Type 1, immediate, IgE-driven. The CLEAR discharge is what separates it at the bedside from bacterial sinusitis, which is purulent. Allergic shiners complete the picture. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which causes are listed as CENTRAL vertigo?","choices":["Brainstem stroke, multiple sclerosis and cerebellar tumour","Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo, Meniere disease and labyrinthitis","Otosclerosis and cerumen impaction","Presbycusis and noise trauma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All involve the brainstem or cerebellum, which is why the nystagmus can be vertical and is not suppressed by fixation, and why other neurological signs appear. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A temporal bone fracture causes conductive loss by which mechanism?","choices":["Discontinuity of the ossicular chain","Stiffness of the ossicular chain","Mass loading of the tympanic membrane","Obstruction of the external canal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The chain is physically disrupted so the wave cannot be conducted across it. Ossicular necrosis produces the same end result by erosion rather than trauma. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What enlarges the inferior turbinates, and what does rhinitis medicamentosa refer to?","choices":["Venous engorgement, oedema or bony hypertrophy; decongestant rebound","Cartilage displacement off the midline; a congenital deformity","Eosinophilic polyp formation; an allergic reaction","Bacterial infection of the turbinate bone; osteomyelitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Three possible substrates, and the drug-induced one has its own name. It is the same rebound phenomenon that appears in ocular pharmacology &mdash; overuse of a vasoconstrictor leaving the tissue more congested than before. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which single event in otosclerosis produces the hearing loss?","choices":["Erosion of the incus by the remodelled bone","Hair cell loss at the cochlear base","Ankylosis of the stapes footplate","Accumulation of endolymph in the scala media"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Once the footplate is fixed it cannot transfer mechanical vibration into the cochlear fluid, and conduction fails progressively. Fixation is the whole lesion. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"How do vocal cord polyps differ from nodules in site and pathogenesis?","choices":["Bilateral fibrous calluses from chronic phonotrauma","Unilateral, soft and pedunculated on the middle third, after one severe strain","Bilateral soft lesions at the anterior commissure from reflux","Unilateral fibrous calluses from chronic yelling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. One event rather than chronic repetition, and the healing response is inflammatory rather than a callus. Site and laterality both differ from nodules. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which organism is the usual bacterial cause of tonsillitis, and which finding suggests abscess?","choices":["Streptococcus pneumoniae; bilateral exudate","Pseudomonas aeruginosa; fever above 39 degrees","Group A Streptococcus; asymmetric deviation with trismus","Haemophilus influenzae; odynophagia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Deviation and trismus indicate the infection has collected rather than staying confined to the tonsil and pharyngeal mucosa. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which vascular source accounts for anterior epistaxis, and what proportion of cases is that?","choices":["Woodruff's plexus on the posterolateral wall, about 90 per cent","The sphenopalatine artery, about 50 per cent","The anterior ethmoidal artery, about 10 per cent","Kiesselbach's plexus on the anterior septum, about 90 per cent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nine in ten nosebleeds are anterior and come from this plexus, which is why direct compression works so often. The posterior 10 per cent come from Woodruff's plexus on the posterolateral wall. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Middle ear effusion and cholesteatoma impair conduction by which mechanism?","choices":["Mass loading","Obstruction","Stiffness effect","Discontinuity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both add fluid or tissue weight that damps movement of the tympanic membrane and ossicles. The chain is intact and mobile in principle, but it is carrying extra mass. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the defect in conductive hearing loss, and where does it sit?","choices":["Destruction of hair cells or auditory nerve fibres, in the inner ear","Defective sound transmission, in the external or middle ear","Failure of glutamate release at cranial nerve VIII","Central processing failure in the auditory cortex"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The apparatus that carries sound inward has failed; the cochlea and nerve are intact. That anatomical split is what the tuning fork tests are detecting. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of Meniere disease?","choices":["Inflammatory swelling and vascular congestion of the labyrinth after a viral infection","Dislodged otoconia floating in the semicircular canals","Defective endolymph resorption causing hydrops, ballooning the scala media until micro-ruptures occur","Demyelination of the vestibular nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Endolymphatic hydrops is the lesion: fluid accumulates because it is not resorbed, the membranous labyrinth distends, and micro-ruptures follow. The episodic nature follows from that cycle. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of labyrinthitis, and why does it impair hearing AND balance together?","choices":["Inflammation across both the semicircular canals and the cochlea","Defective endolymph resorption ballooning the scala media","Dislodged otoconia striking structures in the endolymph","Fixation of the stapes footplate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The inflammation does not respect the boundary between the vestibular apparatus and the organ of Corti, so both fail at once. That simultaneous involvement is what names it otitis interna. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which history most separates labyrinthitis from Meniere disease?","choices":["Unilateral rather than bilateral symptoms","The presence of tinnitus","A recent upper respiratory infection","The presence of sensorineural hearing loss"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Labyrinthitis usually follows a viral infection, so a cold last week points hard at it; someone who has not had a cold in years is likelier to have Meniere disease. Both can be unilateral, so laterality does not separate them. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which way does the nystagmus of labyrinthitis beat?","choices":["Horizontal-rotary, with the fast phase beating TOWARD the affected side","Vertical and non-suppressible","Absent, because hearing rather than balance is affected","Horizontal-rotary, with the fast phase beating AWAY from the affected side"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The damaged side is under-firing, so the slow drift is toward it and the corrective fast phase beats away. Severe postural instability, gait ataxia and nausea come with it. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Depolarisation of the cochlear hair cell comes from an ion influx. Through what, and out of which fluid?","choices":["Efflux into the perilymph through voltage-gated channels","Influx from the perilymph across the basilar membrane","Influx from the endolymph through the tip-link channels","Release from intracellular stores after glutamate binding"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The endolymph is the reservoir, and opening the tip-link channels lets ions rush in. Depolarisation then opens voltage-gated channels and glutamate is released onto cranial nerve VIII. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Above what sound level does long-term chronic exposure cause irreversible cochlear damage, and what is the cellular change?","choices":["Above 85 decibels, causing reversible stereocilia fatigue","Above 85 decibels, by stereocilia degeneration and apoptosis","Above 120 decibels, causing tympanic membrane rupture","Above 60 decibels, causing endolymphatic hydrops"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both words matter: degeneration of the stereocilia and programmed death of the hair cells. Neither regenerates, which is why the loss is permanent. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the demographic and inheritance pattern of otosclerosis?","choices":["Commonest in older males, with no hereditary component","Commonest in children, autosomal recessive","Young-to-middle-aged females, accelerated by pregnancy; 50% autosomal dominant","Commonest in young females but entirely sporadic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pregnancy acceleration is the memorable part, and variable penetrance explains why the family history is often incomplete. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What drives dizziness or lightheadedness, as distinct from vertigo?","choices":["Asymmetrical input between the two vestibular apparatus","Dislodged otoconia in the semicircular canals","Endolymphatic hydrops","Cerebral hypoperfusion or metabolic imbalance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. None of these is vestibular, which is why the sensation is of impending faint rather than of the room moving. Getting this distinction right is the first branch point in the whole workup. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which nodal findings require immediate assessment to exclude lymphoma?","choices":["Tender mobile nodes during a viral upper respiratory infection","Bilateral small nodes in a child with otitis media","Any palpable cervical node at all","Persistent rubbery or matted nodes in older adults"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Reactive nodes are usually tender and follow a regional infection. Rubbery, matted, persistent and supraclavicular in an older patient is the combination that changes the level of concern. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What do the semicircular canals detect, and how?","choices":["Rotational acceleration, by endolymph bending the cupula","Linear acceleration and gravitational position, via the otoliths","Sound frequency, via the basilar membrane","Middle ear pressure, via the Eustachian tube"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Three loops in the sagittal, coronal and transverse planes. The fluid lags behind the head, the gelatinous cupula bends, and the hair cell stereocilia inside it are stimulated. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What defines vertigo, and what distinguishes it from dizziness?","choices":["A sensation of impending faint from cerebral hypoperfusion","A hallucination of motion, with NO syncope","Any unsteadiness on standing","Loss of consciousness preceded by spinning"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Asymmetry between the two sides is the mechanism, and the absence of syncope is the separator. Dizziness or lightheadedness is a non-vestibular sensation of impending faint. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"}]};
